INT. NIGHT BUS - MOVING - NIGHT

the 3am route out of the city. mostly empty. heater ticking. snow in the headlights, getting thicker.

SAM, 30s, sits sideways across two seats with a backpack on his lap. across the aisle, MO, 20s, eats crisps out of a tiny bag.

at the back, THE BAND (four teenagers, guitar cases, one amp) slumps against each other, half asleep.

SAM
is it me or are we going slower

MO
we're going slower

SAM
cool. cool cool cool.

the BUS DRIVER, unseen but for his cap in the mirror, hums along to the radio.

MO
you want one

SAM
what are they

MO
salt and vinegar. i think. the bag's gone soft.

SAM
yeah ok

mo tosses the bag. sam catches it, drops half.

SAM (CONT'D)
sorry

MO
it's fine, sam. it's like, a crisp.

an OLD WOMAN at the front, asleep, a small DOG in her coat. the dog looks at sam. sam looks at the dog.

SAM
that dog hates me

MO
that dog's seen things

THE BAND
(mumbling, in unison, not awake)
ten more minutes.

SAM
they're asleep, right

MO
they're so asleep

the bus slows. slows. the engine note drops.

SAM
mo

MO
yeah

SAM
that's not a stop

the bus rolls to a halt in the middle of a white nothing. the driver cuts the engine. the heater stops ticking.

quiet. the radio hums on batteries.

THE BAND
(waking, in unison)
are we there

MO
not really, no

two TEENAGERS in school hoodies, a row ahead, look up from a shared pair of earbuds. look out the window. look back down.

SAM
should we be worried

MO
i think we should be a little worried

SAM
how much is a little

MO
like. a sixth.

SAM
a sixth. ok. i can do a sixth.

the driver stands, pulls on a coat.

DRIVER (O.S.)
folks, bit of snow on the road. stay put. i'll radio in.

he steps off. the doors sigh. cold rolls down the aisle.

MO
he's going to radio in

SAM
from where

MO
from outside, i guess

SAM
this is not a radio situation

MO
sam

SAM
what

MO
you're being weird. it's fine. people get stuck. buses get unstuck.

SAM
yeah

this is the last quiet moment before the night gets long.

THE BAND
(whispered, in unison)
can we have the heater back

SAM
i'm so sorry. the heater is gone.

MO
we'll take turns

SAM
turns doing what

MO
being warm. it's a thing. you pass it.

sam laughs, a little, then stops.

the old woman wakes up. looks around. says nothing. shuts her eyes again. the dog sighs.

SAM
mo

MO
yeah

SAM
if this goes bad, i just want you to know i like your coat

MO
it's my brother's coat

SAM
i like him too

mo throws a crisp at him. it hits his forehead. he leaves it there.

outside, the snow keeps falling. the road is gone. the windows fog.

MO
big dave's gonna be so mad i'm late

SAM
who's big dave

MO
my boss. he's not even big. we just have two daves.

SAM
that's the worst reason

MO
it's the only reason

the driver climbs back on, shoulders white.

DRIVER
okay. we wait.

he sits. takes off the cap. wipes his face.

nobody speaks. the dog snores. somewhere in the dark, a plough, maybe, or just wind.

SAM
mo

MO
yeah

SAM
thanks for the crisps

MO
you didn't eat them

SAM
i held them very respectfully

the bus creaks. the snow settles. they wait.
